Yes — 3D models lie at the heart of what we now call **digital twins**, and they’re already being used across ST Engineering’s businesses to simulate wear, fatigue and predict maintenance. A few concrete examples:

* **Aerospace Predictive Maintenance**
  Aircraft digital twins ingest real-time sensor feeds (temperature, vibration, cycle counts) and run physics-based simulations to estimate **bearing fatigue**, **metal crack propagation**, and **remaining useful life**. Engineers can “drive” the virtual engine under different loads and see exactly when parts would wear out—so you schedule shop visits before AOG (Aircraft on Ground) ever happens ([AltexSoft][1], [attinc.com][2]).

* **Manufacturing Process & Tool Wear**
  In ST Engineering’s **Manufacturing Simulation Centre**, every machine, tool and conveyor is represented as a 3D virtual twin. By simulating thousands of production cycles, they can predict **tool-bit wear**, **surface-finish degradation**, and **jig-alignment drift**, then adjust tooling paths or maintenance intervals **before defects occur** ([ST Engineering][3]).

* **Port-Crane Structural Fatigue**
  For PSA’s container-handling cranes, digital twins combine the 3D CAD of booms and hoists with load-history data. Finite-element modules then simulate **metal fatigue** in welds and joints under repetitive lifts—so they replace or reinforce components **just in time** rather than waiting for a surprise breakdown ([AltexSoft][1]).

* **Rail & Tunnel Asset Health**
  In projects with LTA, 3D models of tracks, sleepers and tunnel linings feed into digital-twin platforms that run **wear-and-tear simulations** under passenger-load and vibration data. This flags sections where rail profiles need grinding or concrete segments require sealing, turning reactive “track-work” into proactive maintenance.

---

**Bottom line:** Everywhere ST Engineering operates—aircraft MRO, smart manufacturing, ports, rail infrastructure—they’re already using **3D-model-based digital twins** (not just static CAD) to **simulate wear and tear**, **optimize maintenance**, and **extend asset lifecycles**.

