Yes — you’ve hit on the core barrier: an end-to-end “prompt→3D CAD→FEA→CAM→digital-twin” pipeline hinges on **massive, high-fidelity proprietary data** (maintenance logs, sensor streams, failure histories, tooling records, etc.).

1. **Startups Lack the Data**

   * Without years of in-service logs or live IoT feeds, any AI-CAD or digital-twin proof-of-concept is trained on generic, public datasets—far too noisy or limited to drive production-grade models.
   * They simply can’t validate that “3 photos → CAD solid in 5 minutes” will hold up under your actual load profiles and failure modes. ([NIST][1])

2. **Corporates Guard Their Data**

   * Large operators (airlines, ports, utilities, defence) treat their logs and sensor streams as **mission-critical IP**. They won’t hand over raw data to unproven vendors for fear of leaks, compliance breaches or loss of competitive advantage. ([SpringerLink][2])

3. **Only a Few Can Do It Today**

   * **Tier-1 System Integrators & Operators** with deep in-house archives—ST Engineering, Siemens, PTC, Dassault Systèmes, GE Digital, etc.—are essentially the only organisations today able to stitch together every link of that chain.
   * They both **own the data** and have the **engineering muscle** to automate CAD, FEA, CAM and run full digital-twin lifecycles without manual handoffs.

---

### Exception: Data Collaboratives & Partnerships

* Some consortia (“data collaboratives”) let startups access sanitized slices of proprietary data under tight NDAs and usage controls—enough to train or validate narrowly scoped AI modules, though never the full, live-asset pipeline. ([Wikipedia][3])
* In practice, most startups succeed by partnering closely with one anchor customer—getting limited data access, building a killer module, then expanding as they prove value.

---

**Bottom line:** You’re right that, **absent privileged data**, no startup today can deliver a truly autonomous, end-to-end prompt-to-production digital-twin platform. That capability sits squarely in the hands of a **very small handful of super-data-rich integrators**.

